.\" https://scrn.w0zro.com/guide tells the same story in walking order;
.\" this page is the reference. Keep the two agreeing.
.TH SCRN 1 2026-08-30 scrn "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scrn \- a terminal UI for working on projects at the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scrn
.br
.B scrn ls
.br
.B scrn
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scrn
shows every git repository under your project roots, what is running in
each, and the shells you open there. The shells live in a
.BR tmux (1)
server of scrn's own, so they survive the window: close the terminal,
come back, and everything is where it was.
.PP
There is nothing to set up beyond having tmux installed. The first shell
brings the server up \(em private to scrn, on its own socket, out of
reach of any .tmux.conf \(em and every window attaches to it. By
default scrn looks for repositories under
.IR ~/projects ;
the config points it elsewhere.
.PP
The left column is a tree, at most four levels deep: group, repository,
sub\-project, process. A folder holding two or more repositories groups
them. In a monorepo, a directory with a manifest
.RI ( .scrn ,
.IR Procfile ,
.IR package.json ,
.IR go.mod ,
.IR Cargo.toml ,
and the like) is a project of its own. Processes are attributed to the
innermost place they belong to and named by what you would call them \(em
.BR "npm run dev" ,
not
.BR node .
Dim rows are processes started outside scrn: listed and killable, but
with no shell to step into.
.PP
.B scrn ls
is the same server's state for a reader that is not a person: the held
shells, one per line on stdout \(em pid, directory and name,
tab\-separated, the name empty for a shell opened by hand. No server
listening means nothing is held, which is an empty list, not an error.
.SH KEYS
.B ?
shows this list inside scrn. The foot of the column wears the mode the
keys are in, the way vim says INSERT:
.B navigate
at the list,
.B proc
while the keys are inside a shell or its transcript,
.B prefix
while a chord hangs. The mode keeps the bottom line to itself; anything
scrn has to say \(em a report, an error, a confirmation \(em appears
above it.
.SS In the navigator
.TP
.B "j k"
move; the arrows and tab move too
.TP
.B "gg G"
jump to the top and the bottom
.TP
.B s
open a shell at the selected place
.TP
.B a
start an agent there \(em Claude Code, unless the config's
.B agent
says otherwise
.TP
.B A
continue a suspended conversation there: the place's past agent sessions,
newest first and searchable
.TP
.B r
run the plan's entries that are not already running
.TP
.B enter
step into the selected shell
.TP
.B space
fold or unfold the selected node
.TP
.B \-
fold or unfold everything; unfolded rows also show pids
.TP
.B .
toggle between everything and only what is running
.TP
.B /
find by name: repositories, and the processes whose commands answer
.TP
.B x
kill the selected process; on a repository or group, everything running
in it
.TP
.B X
kill the selected process and everything under it
.TP
.B R
end the server outright, and every shell it holds with it
.TP
.B q
quit the window; the shells keep running
.PP
Every kill asks first \(em
.BR x ,
.B y
or
.B enter
confirms, anything else cancels. Processes get SIGTERM, and the list
never claims an exit it has not observed.
.SS In the filter
Letters type, and the list narrows under a cursor that
.B up
and
.B down
(or
.B ctrl\-p
and
.BR ctrl\-n )
move. A query lists the processes whose commands answer it as well as the
places, pruned to the branches that answer; an empty query lists places
alone. Acting on what the cursor is on ends the looking:
.B enter
opens a shell on a place and steps into a found process's shell,
.B ctrl\-r
runs the plan there,
.B ctrl\-a
starts an agent, and
.B ctrl\-x
asks to kill \(em the filter holds, so the subject stays listed.
.B ctrl\-u
clears the query, and
.B esc
abandons the look, putting things back as they were: the cursor on the
row it left, or the keys in the shell they came from.
.SS In a shell
Keystrokes go to the shell. scrn keeps one key for itself:
.TP
.B ctrl\-space
the prefix; it reaches scrn from anywhere, even a focused shell, and
every other keystroke belongs to the pane
.PP
cmd\-v pastes even in a terminal that hands the chord to scrn instead
of translating it: the clipboard is read, and the paste lands wherever
typing goes.
.PP
The chords keep their letters' meanings:
.B n
steps back out to the navigator,
.B j
and
.B k
attach to the next and previous shell, another
.B ctrl\-space
toggles back to the shell viewed before,
.B enter
goes to the next agent waiting on you,
.B /
opens the filter,
.BR s ,
.BR a ,
.B A
and
.B r
act where you are,
.B q
quits, and
.B ?
shows the keys. Anything unbound cancels the prefix.
.PP
The mouse belongs to whatever the pane is showing, entered or merely
selected: the wheel scrolls a preview's transcript or the program in
it, and a click on a preview steps into its shell, the way clicking an
unfocused window focuses it.
scrn mostly does not hold the mouse at all: dragging, double\-clicking
and copying are the terminal's own, everywhere, exactly as they are
with no scrn in the middle. Only while the focused program has asked
for the mouse \(em vim, htop \(em does scrn take it, and then every
event crosses raw: press, drag, release, wheel. While it is held, the
navigator still takes a click and the wheel. The terminal's own selection also works, with shift held while
dragging, and the reader below copies by keyboard alone. A program that copies for itself \(em vim's
clipboard yank, anything speaking OSC 52 \(em reaches the system
clipboard the way it would with no scrn in the middle.
.PP
.B ctrl\-space v
opens the reader over what has scrolled away \(em the pager's screen
included.
.B j
and
.B k
move,
.B v
marks the bottom line and moving extends the mark,
.B y
copies the marked lines to the system clipboard, and
.B esc
puts down the mark, then the reading. Each shell keeps 10,000 lines of
transcript unless the config says otherwise.
.SH AGENTS
.B a
starts an agent in a shell like any other. scrn knows agents as kinds
\(em claude and ollama today \(em and the kinds are unequal on
purpose: each shows only what its tool actually advertises. Claude
Code advertises everything; scrn reads the session files it leaves
behind, and the navigator marks each instance's state: a spinner while it works, a diamond (\[u25C6]) when it is blocked
mid\-turn on a permission prompt or a question, a filled dot (\[u25CF])
when it has finished its turn, a hollow dot (\[u25CB]) when it has been
idle since it started. The diamond is the one worth crossing the room
for: answering it resumes work already underway.
.B ctrl\-space enter
goes to the next agent waiting on you, from wherever the keys were
going; pressed again it continues around the waiting ones in turn.
.PP
A conversation does not end when its instance exits; it is suspended,
and the transcript Claude keeps is enough to pick it back up.
.B A
on a place lists what is suspended there \(em including its
sub\-projects, and for a group its repositories \(em newest first: when
each conversation last moved, the branch it was on, and the last thing
asked of it. Letters narrow the list the way the filter narrows,
.B up
and
.B down
move,
.B enter
continues the one under the cursor \(em a shell opens where the
conversation was had, running
.BR "claude --resume" ,
and from there it is a live instance like any other \(em and
.B esc
closes the list. A conversation an instance is still carrying is not
offered. Every kind that keeps conversations answers the picker, and
each is continued by the kind that had it.
.PP
A kind that advertises nothing \(em an ollama REPL \(em is still an
agent to start: its rows are plain processes, unmarked, which is the
whole truth about them.
.SH PLANS
A project writes down its usual processes in
.IR .scrn ,
Procfile format, a name and a command per line:
.PP
.EX
dev:   npm run dev
api:   go run ./cmd/api
agent: claude
.EE
.PP
Without one, a
.I Procfile
means every line is a process, and a
.I package.json
contributes its
.B dev
or
.B start
script, whichever exists first.
.B r
starts the entries that are not running, each in its own shell named for
its entry. It is a list to run, not a supervisor: nothing restarts on its
own.
.SH CONFIGURATION
One JSON file, entirely optional, at
.IR ~/.config/scrn/config.json .
.TP
.B projectsDir
the directory searched for repositories (default
.IR $HOME/projects );
.B ~
and
.B $VARS
expand
.TP
.B projectsDirs
several roots; when set,
.B projectsDir
is ignored. A root that only exists on another machine is fine.
.TP
.B scrollback
transcript lines each shell keeps (default 10000). Settled into the
server as the first shell brings it up, so raising it takes
.B R
\(em a fresh server \(em to reach anything.
.TP
.B navWidth
the navigator column's width, 16\(en60 (default 28)
.TP
.B agent
the kind of agent
.B a
starts:
.B claude
(the default) or
.BR ollama .
A name scrn does not know falls back to the default
.TP
.B agentRuns
what starting a kind runs, by name, when its own answer is not the
wanted one \(em
.B {"ollama": "ollama run mistral"}
picks the model ollama would otherwise guess at (its first listed)
.TP
.B skipDirs
directory names the repository scan never enters, on top of the built\-in
list
.RI ( node_modules ,
.IR vendor ,
.IR target ,
and the like)
.SH THE SERVER
The shells live in a tmux server of scrn's own: it owns the ptys, the
emulation and the transcripts, and every window talks to it \(em one
server per user, for all projects, on a socket private to scrn where no
.I .tmux.conf
reaches. Keys, modes, mouse reporting, the alternate screen: all of it
behaves the way it behaves in tmux, because it is tmux.
.PP
The server lives exactly as long as it holds something: the first shell
brings it up, the last one closing takes it down. Upgrading scrn never
disturbs it \(em the server is not scrn's binary, so a new build talks
to the same server and the shells never notice.
.PP
In a pinch the shells are reachable without scrn at all:
.EX
tmux -S ~/.local/state/scrn/tmux-<uid>.sock attach
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/scrn/config.json
configuration; see
.B CONFIGURATION
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/scrn/tmux\-<uid>.sock
the socket of the tmux server holding the shells
.TP
.I .scrn
a project's plan; see
.B PLANS
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SCRN_SOCKET
where the server listens, instead of the state directory
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
where the config lives, instead of
.I ~/.config
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
where the server's socket lives, instead of
.I ~/.local/state
.TP
.B SHELL
the shell opened in each pane
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
set, scrn draws without color, as
.UR https://no\-color.org
the convention
.UE
asks.
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
and
.B TERM=dumb
are honored too.
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
where Claude Code's session files are read from, instead of
.I ~/.claude
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a clean exit, 1 when scrn or its server fails, 2 for an argument it
does not understand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR lsof (1),
.BR git (1)
